Rakazo is the project Wingman was originally scoped against, and its visual
language is adopted in web/ rather than re-invented. Wingman is distributed
under the same licence, so the copy of it required by section 4(c) is the
LICENSE file beside this one. Rakazo ships no NOTICE of its own, so there are no
upstream attribution notices to carry forward. What is adapted, and where:

  web/app/globals.css        the dark palette, adopted slot for slot with the
                             hex values verbatim, and the 12px-base radius
                             scale
  web/app/avatar.css         ported from packages/ui-web/src/styles.css
  web/components/bot-avatar.tsx
                             ported from packages/ui-web/src/bot-avatar.tsx,
                             including the seven bot colours and the string
                             hash unchanged, and the wordmark's shape
  web/components/rail.tsx    the sidebar composition, and its off-canvas
                             drawer below the desktop breakpoint
  web/components/chat.tsx    the chat treatment: bubbles, the face beside the
                             agent's message, the round composer
  web/components/chrome.tsx  the card surface model — own fill, one hairline,
                             a 12px corner

Changes are stated in the header of each file above, as section 4(b) asks. In
summary: the avatar renders on the server, so it carries no useId and no SVG
gradient; only the robot face is ported, not the generated organic one; the
palette has three documented exceptions and one more level of text than Rakazo
has, because this console has dense tables and an 11px caption over every
reading; and the wordmark is set in Geist rather than Rakazo's Aeonik, which is
licensed and cannot ship in an open-source repository.

Wingman depends on third-party libraries, each under its own licence and none of
them vendored. The authoritative list is go.mod and go.sum in each module, and
web/package.json for the console. The ones distributed under the Apache License,
Version 2.0, and therefore named here:

  github.com/openai/openai-go            Apache-2.0
  github.com/modelcontextprotocol/go-sdk Apache-2.0 and MIT (a relicensing
                                         transition; both halves permissive)

TypeScript is also Apache-2.0, and is named separately because it is build
tooling: the compiler type-checks the console and is in no shipped artifact.

The remaining Go dependencies are MIT or BSD licensed: the Anthropic Go SDK, gin,
sqlx, lib/pq, golang-migrate, zerolog, godotenv, go-sqlmock, google/uuid,
yaml.v3, protobuf and the golang.org/x packages. On the console side, Next,
React, Tailwind CSS and Vitest are MIT.

  Geist and Geist Mono                   SIL Open Font License, Version 1.1

Named here because the console does distribute them, even though neither is a
package in web/package.json: next/font fetches both at build time and serves them
from the console's own origin, deliberately, so that a self-hosted ops console
does not render differently when its network is broken.

